Q: In C# what is the best way to detect that an optional parameter wasn&#39;t specified? A: <h2>TL;DR</h2>
<p>You can skip to the section <strong>Using <code>Optional&lt;T&gt;</code> design pattern</strong> below.</p>
<h2>No universal built-in sentinel value</h2>
<p>C# does not have a built-in universal sentinal value for optional arguments, such as <code>undefined</code> in JavaScript.
We can use default value for an optional parameter, such as:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public static void SomeMethod(string arg1, string arg2 = &quot;&quot;)
{
    //...
}
</code></pre>
<p>However, that does not guarantee in the receiving function or method that <code>&quot;&quot;</code> indeed is not given from the caller:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>SomeMethod(&quot;a&quot;, &quot;&quot;); // arg2 is given as &quot;&quot;
SomeMethod(&quot;b&quot;); // arg2 is missing
</code></pre>
<p>A test inside <code>SomeMethod()</code> with <code>if (arg2 == &quot;&quot;)</code> cannot tell the difference.</p>
<p>A common solution is to leverage the <strong>overloading</strong> of methods / functions. That is, write 2 versions of the same method/function signature, like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public static void SomeMethod(string arg1, string arg2)
{
    //...
}

public static void SomeMethod(string arg1, string arg2 = &quot;&quot;)
{
    //...
}
</code></pre>
<p>But there are times when we really don't want to write 2 versions of the same code just to test if the argument is missing, aka the <strong>DRY</strong> principle.</p>
<h2>Another common approach for value type default arguments</h2>
<p>If the argument type is a value type such as primitive type (<code>int</code>, <code>char</code>, <code>double</code>, etc) or <code>struct</code>, then we can define the
parameter with a nullable value type. Like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public static void SomeMethod(int arg1, int? arg2 = null)
{
    if (arg2 is null)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(&quot;arg2 is missing.&quot;);
    }
    else
    {
        Console.WriteLine(arg2);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>We can try to call the method this way:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>SomeMethod(10, 20); // displays: 20
SomeMethod(30); // displays: arg2 is missing.
</code></pre>
<p>But it is not watertight:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>SomeMethod(40, null); // displays: arg2 is missing.
</code></pre>
<p>One again this can be solved by writing 2 versions of <code>SameMethod()</code> and one of them doesn't have the optional argument, but it's still against the DRY principle. Also, using overloading has a bigger issue... What if there are multiple optional arguments?</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public static void SomeMethod(int? arg1 = null, int? arg2 = null, int? arg3 = null)
{
    //...
}
</code></pre>
<p>Clearly it would be a maintenance issue when we need to write so many overloaded versions just to check if the arguments are missing (using default).</p>
<h2>Using <code>Optional&lt;T&gt;</code> design pattern</h2>
<p>We can define a generic <code>Optional&lt;T&gt;</code> struct, which is a common design pattern in functional programming, like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public readonly struct Optional&lt;T&gt;
{
    private readonly T _value;

    public bool HasValue { get; } = false;

    public T Value =&gt; HasValue ? _value : throw new InvalidOperationException(&quot;No value.&quot;);

    private Optional(T value)
    {
        _value = value;
        HasValue = true;
    }

    public static Optional&lt;T&gt; Some(T value) =&gt; new(value);

    public static implicit operator Optional&lt;T&gt;(T value) =&gt; Some(value);
}
</code></pre>
<p>The generic type parameter <code>T</code> can be a value type or a reference type.
The constuctor is hidden with <code>private</code> keyword because we want to avoid writing such ugly code (if it stays <code>public</code>):</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>Optional&lt;int&gt; value1 = new Optional&lt;int&gt;(100);
Optional&lt;int&gt; value2 = new(200);
</code></pre>
<p>Instead, a static function <code>Some()</code> is there to convert the value of the type argument into the <code>Optional</code> object. And then an implicit operator is provided
to perform that conversion as a syntatic sugar, so we can write something like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>Optional&lt;int&gt; value3 = 300;
</code></pre>
<p>We are now able to define a universal sentinel value for default argument this way:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public static void SomeMethod(int arg1, Optional&lt;int&gt; arg2 = default)
{
    if (!arg2.HasValue)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(&quot;arg2 is missing.&quot;);
    }
    else
    {
        Console.WriteLine(arg2.Value);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Test result:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>SomeMethod(10, 20); // displays: 20
SomeMethod(30); // displays: arg2 is missing.
</code></pre>
<p>This looks the same as the original way which uses the nullable type solution. However, the following test reveals the difference:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>SomeMethod(40, null); // error: CS1503: Argument 2: cannot convert from '&lt;null&gt;' to 'Optional&lt;int&gt;'
</code></pre>
<h2>Using Roslyn's <code>Optional&lt;T&gt;</code></h2>
<p>Since <code>Optional&lt;T&gt;</code> is such a useful design pattern, Roslyn comes with a version so you don't need to reinvent the wheel.</p>
<p>To use it:</p>
<ol>
<li>Start Visual Studio 2026 -&gt; open project -&gt; Solution Explorer -&gt; right-mouse click project node -&gt; select &quot;Manage NuGet Packages...&quot;</li>
<li>Select &quot;Browse&quot; tab, in search box type &quot;Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Common&quot;, install the found package of the exact name by &quot;Microsoft, RoslynTeam&quot;.</li>
</ol>
<p>In the C# code add the following line at the beginning:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis;
</code></pre>
<p>And then we can remove our own ad hoc implementation of <code>Optional&lt;T&gt;</code>.</p>
